    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:1",
      "id": "thm-burnsides-p-a-q-b-theorem",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 says that when one exponent is zero, applying [F2] to G trianglelefteq G gives a nontrivial central subgroup and then induction with [F3] proves solvability. But [F2] only states that every nontrivial normal subgroup of a finite p-group meets its center nontrivially. For G=C_q (a=0, b=1), this yields Z(G)=G, not a proper smaller normal subgroup, so neither minimality nor induction can be invoked through [F3]. The missing abelian/base-case branch is needed.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
